    <h2>Which calculator answers which question</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>"What is this option worth, and what happens if the stock moves?"</strong> The <a href="../../calculators/options.html">options calculator</a> prices calls, puts, and spreads of up to 4 legs and shows how value, Greeks, and profit change with price, time, and implied volatility.</li>
      <li><strong>"How much income does selling a covered call or cash-secured put produce?"</strong> The <a href="../../calculators/covered-call-cash-secured-put.html">covered call and cash-secured put calculator</a> gives premium yield, annualized return, breakeven, and the return if assigned.</li>
      <li><strong>"How far is the market pricing this stock to move?"</strong> The <a href="../../calculators/expected-move.html">expected move calculator</a> turns implied volatility or an at-the-money straddle into a dollar range.</li>
      <li><strong>"Did my wheel or roll actually make money?"</strong> The <a href="../../calculators/options-roll-wheel.html">roll and wheel tracker</a> adds up every credit and debit in a campaign and shows the true breakeven.</li>
      <li><strong>"What does steady investing grow to?"</strong> The <a href="../../calculators/compound-interest.html">compound interest calculator</a> projects growth with regular contributions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Implied volatility in 1 formula</h2>
    <p>Implied volatility is the market's annualized estimate of how much a stock may move. To turn it into a dollar range over a shorter period, scale by the square root of time: expected move ≈ price × IV × √(days ÷ 365). A $100 stock with 30% implied volatility has a 30-day expected move of about $100 × 0.30 × √(30/365) = $8.60. Roughly 2 times in 3, under the model's assumptions, the stock finishes within that range. Earnings announcements and other events can make actual moves much larger.</p>

    <h2>Read premium yields carefully</h2>
    <p>Selling a 30-day covered call for $1.20 on a $50 stock is a 2.4% return for the month, which annualizes to 29.2%. That figure assumes you can repeat the trade every month at the same premium, which rarely holds. It also ignores the trade-off: the call caps your upside at the strike, while you keep all of the stock's downside, reduced only by the premium. Annualized yields are useful for comparing trades with different expirations, not as a forecast of what you will earn in a year.</p>

    <h2>What models can't tell you</h2>
    <p>Option prices here come from standard pricing models that assume volatility stays constant and prices move smoothly. Real markets have gaps, dividends, early assignment on American-style options, and bid-ask spreads that eat into small trades. Use the calculators to understand how a position behaves, then check the real bid and ask in your brokerage before placing an order. Options involve significant risk and are not suitable for every investor.</p>
